  "decline_reason": "",
  "claims": [
    {
      "text": "The relevant people are consumers of the goods or services: a place reference can create a connotation—an associated meaning—in the minds of that buying public.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part29/2/2/2",
          "quote": "A connotation may result from the whole trade mark or from a part of the trade mark. It may result from a dictionary meaning or from a connection in the minds of the buying public with some person, place or thing."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "The Manual says a standalone geographical reference will cause confusion where consumers have reason to associate the goods or services with that place, the association would influence an average consumer’s purchasing decision, and no other elements or surrounding circumstances mitigate the risk.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part29/9/9/3~1",
          "quote": "A trade mark containing a standalone geographical reference will cause confusion if each of the following criteria are met: Consumers would have reason to associate the goods/services with the geographical location; The association between the place and the goods/services would influence the purchasing decision of an average consumer of the goods/services. There are no other elements in the mark, or surrounding circumstances, which would mitigate the risk of confusion."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "In assessing shoppers’ perceptions, the Manual includes how the goods or services are purchased, the goods’ physical characteristics and other relevant surrounding circumstances.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part29/9/9/3~1",
          "quote": "Surrounding circumstances include the manner in which the goods/services are purchased, the physical characteristics of goods and any other factor which would affect consumer perceptions."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "In the Manual’s TUSCAN EFFECT paint example, EFFECT changes the suggested meaning from geographical origin to style: consumers are unlikely to think the paint comes from Tuscany, but instead expect a Tuscan-style surface finish.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part29/9/9/3~2",
          "quote": "Although the trade mark contains a reference to Tuscany, the word EFFECT changes the connotation of the trade mark. Consumers are not likely to think the paint originates from Tuscany, but rather that the paint will give a surface a Tuscan-style finish."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "The legal consequence under section 43 is mandatory rejection if, because of a connotation of the mark or a sign within it, use for the particular goods or services would be likely to deceive or cause confusion.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMA1995/s43",
          "quote": "An application for the registration of a trade mark in respect of particular goods or services must be rejected if, because of some connotation that the trade mark or a sign contained in the trade mark has, the use of the trade mark in relation to those goods or services would be likely to deceive or cause confusion."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "legislation"
    }
  ],
